      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm hitting an \"Access Denied\" error with an S3 bucket. im trying to let a lambda function write logs to it but the policy doesnt seem to be working. can you look at this json and tell me what im doing wrong?\n\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Action\": [\n        \"s3:PutObject\"\n      ],\n      \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:s3:::my-log-bucket\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The issue with your JSON policy is the action \"s3:PutObject\". Lambda functions need permission to write logs to S3, not put objects. Instead, specify the \"s3:PutObject\" action on the log bucket name, which typically follows the format: \"arn:aws:s3:::my-log-bucket/AWSLogs/<account-id>/<prefix>\".\n\nAdditionally, ensure you are using the correct account ID and log stream prefix for your application logs. You should also check if service-linked roles or assume role policies need to be in place for Lambda integrations with S3. Here's an updated snippet:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Action\": \"s3:PutObject\",\n      \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:s3:::my-log-bucket/AWSLogs/my-application-id/lambda\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nReplace \"my-application-id\" and \"lambda\" with the appropriate identifiers for your setup.",
      "source": "target",
