      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for the snippet. i tried adding the path like you said but i'm still getting an access denied when the lambda runs. is it possible i need permissions on the bucket itself and not just the objects? like s3:ListBucket or something? i'm getting really frustrated with these permission errors.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In the situation where your Lambda function is encountering an \"access denied,\" aside from permissions on objects within the S3 bucket you're targeting, you might indeed lack permissions needed for additional actions like \"s3:ListBucket.\"\n\nTo write logs in S3, your Lambda function generally doesn't need \"s3:ListBucket\" permissions. Lambda needs write access to place logs into an unsupported bucket directly via \"s3:PutObject\" on the target bucket, including the correct key and prefix, as indicated earlier.\n\nLet's make sure your permissions are set correctly:\n\n1. Verify you have the correct S3 bucket name and prefix for your Lambda function logs.\n2. Ensure your IAM role attached to Lambda contains a policy with the right octal permissions (permissions boundary).\n\nHere's an example for a Lambda execution role policy:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Action\": \"s3:PutObject\",\n      \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:s3:::my-log-bucket/AWSLogs/my-application-id/lambda/*\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nRemember to understand this JSON policy: it grants permission to your Lambda function",
      "source": "target",
